// Practice quiz: Access modifiers
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/access-modifiers

::o1 package access::A method is declared with no access modifier. Which code can call it? {
=Only code in classes of the same package#Correct. Package access, the default when you write no modifier, stops at the package boundary.
~Any class that imports its class#Not quite. An import only shortens names; it does not widen access.
~Only code inside its own class#Not quite. That is private access.
~Code in the same package and in subclasses in other packages#Not quite. That is protected access, which adds subclasses on top of the package.
}

::o1 top-level class::Which access modifiers can a top-level class, one not nested inside another class, be declared with? {
=public, or no modifier for package access#Correct. A top-level class is either visible everywhere or only within its package.
~Any of private, protected, package and public#Not quite. private and protected apply to members and nested classes, not to a top-level class.
~Only public#Not quite. A top-level class may also have package access by leaving out the modifier.
~Only private#Not quite. A private top-level class would be unusable, so the compiler rejects it.
}

::o2 hidden class::In package tools\: class Helper \{ public void run() \{\} \}. In package app\: new tools.Helper().run(); Does the call compile? {
=No, because class Helper itself has package access#Correct. A public method is only reachable when its class is also visible; Helper has no modifier, so app cannot see it.
~Yes, because run is public#Not quite. The method is public, but the class around it is not visible outside tools.
~Yes, because the full name tools.Helper is used#Not quite. A fully qualified name does not get around access rules.
~No, because the call needs an import statement#Not quite. A fully qualified name works without an import when the class is visible; visibility is the problem here.
}

::o2 protected outside::In package shop\: public class Base \{ protected int count; \}. In package report, class Summary does not extend Base and runs new Base().count. Does it compile? {
=No; outside its package a protected member is reachable only from subclasses#Correct. Summary is in another package and is not a subclass, so count is not accessible.
~Yes, protected works like public#Not quite. protected is narrower than public\: other packages get access only through subclasses.
~Yes, because Base is public#Not quite. A public class can still have members that other packages cannot use.
~No, because protected means the same as private#Not quite. protected is wider than private\: it includes the whole package and subclasses.
}

::o3 own helper::A method is called only by other methods of its own class. Which modifier is the most restrictive one that still works? {
=private#Correct. Nothing outside the class needs it, so private hides it completely.
~No modifier, package access#Not quite. That also works, but it opens the method to the whole package for no reason.
~protected#Not quite. That opens the method to the package and to subclasses, which do not need it.
~public#Not quite. That opens the method to every class.
}

::o3 package only::A method is called by other classes in the same package and by no class anywhere else. Which modifier is the narrowest that works? {
=No modifier, package access#Correct. Package access covers exactly the classes in the same package.
~private#Not quite. The other classes in the package could no longer call it.
~protected#Not quite. That also admits subclasses in other packages, which do not need it.
~public#Not quite. That opens it to every package.
}
